1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:03:38,740
Part 6 stopped on an unfinished sentence. For this reason the child was saved. Somehow. How he was saved is worth thinking about. The cowherds had seen Putana's vast body. The dreadful teeth, the eyes, the nose, they had seen it all. A child of six days had come into that rakshasi's grip, and still he was saved. The word kathancit, somehow, gives the devotees a chance to think. This is the subject of Upananda's heart. Somehow he was saved. Ahead they will say, by the mercy of Lord Hari. But everywhere the point is the same: by our fortune, by the Lord's mercy. What was that meeting of their own people? In truth a katha of Krishna was going on, for Krishna's good. By Hari's mercy. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.24] He was saved from the attack of Putana, the rakshasi who kills children. By the mercy of Hari, whose name brings good, who destroys calamities. And that cart fell from above, and it did not fall on the child. With his own soft feet Krishna had overturned the cart. But until now Upananda does not accept that. No one will ever come to know it. The cart fell. Some ghost must have thrown it down. But look, it did not fall on the child. One acharya writes, in what ignorance the cowherds are. They do not know that it failed to fall by Krishna's mercy. It fell by his mercy itself. This ignorance is from the energy of his own form. Love itself makes them forget, and that forgetfulness is their real position. Once he says somehow: somehow he was freed from that child-killing rakshasi. And by Hari's mercy the cart did not fall on this child. The child is the same one. Pointing, he says, the boy was saved.

2
00:03:45,760 --> 00:06:01,440
And from that whirlwind. By the demon who had come as a whirlwind, this Krishna was taken into a very high sky. Vipad. Vi also means a bird, and pada the place of the bird. Where the birds fly, that is called vipad. Or a place with no footing, the place of danger. He took him into danger. He took him to the place of the birds. And where did he fall? Upon a stone. Saved by the Lord of the gods. Lord Vishnu protected the child. Here suresha is plural, for the Lord, out of respect. The Lord had thrown Trinavarta onto the stone. The cowherds had not even touched him with a stone. Holding his throat, they had fallen in fear. He fell, and the body was torn apart. Trinavarta became a whirlwind in many pieces. But they do not know all those things. When the Lord is already saving him, he will save him ahead also. Why the worry. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.25]

3
00:06:01,700 --> 00:09:08,060
When he is being saved, he will be saved ahead also. But the hearts of kinsmen suspect misfortune. Kalidasa has said that the heart of a relative keeps fearing a misfortune. [Kalidasa] Where there is love, the fear of that person's misfortune stays full. See a dear one off on a train. The mind suspects, let there be no accident. Nothing will happen. But one's own dear one is going, so such a fear comes in the heart. Krishna is dearer than life. And look, just now. It is a matter of only six or seven days. Falling between two vast trees, even as they fell, this child did not die. And they had branches spread so wide. Many hundreds of children would have died, crushed. This child did not die. And no other child died either. This too is a protection done by the infallible one. By Krishna. There is great fear in the mind, so a word like dead is being used. Such a word should not come for the Lord. But there is affection. The child did not die. The Lord saved him. At the fall of trees this large, even elephants die, crushed. This one small child, between the two trees. Here too, see the Lord's mercy. They knew that your own child threw them down. Will he let anyone die? He will throw them down with a show of force. A meeting like this, where the heart is full of love for the Lord, and they speak among themselves of the Lord's protection, how beautiful a meeting it will be. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.26]

4
00:09:10,240 --> 00:12:06,090
He placed the proposal that this place should be left. After that he is counting the troubles. When will a trouble come, when will a calamity start. No one stays settled. Nothing is known beforehand. Who knew that these two trees would fall. Someone comes and throws them, and then the trees fall. That is called a misfortune that has a cause. Someone is set upon killing the child. It has a cause. So before some new disturbance comes, we should leave this place and go. We should flee before any doubt. Taking all the children. Taking the boys. He is speaking in the plural. Taking the children, taking Krishna. With the priests, with the servants, with the cows, the whole community is to flee from here. We are not to stay here. There is this much anxiety for Krishna's protection. Until a new place is decided, the old place should not be left. You are saying Mahavana should be left. But where will we stay. First the place should be fixed, and then one's own house should be left. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.27]

5
00:12:07,360 --> 00:16:56,330
This is accepted at once. Because all of them, like Upananda, were full of vatsalya for Krishna. All were experiencing that a great danger is coming here. The elder cowherds had wandered the whole circle of Braj many times. They knew which place is of what kind. So he says, there is a forest named Vrindavan. It is good for animals. The good of the animals will happen there. What does an animal need. Grass and water. This is the main point. There is abundant grass there, and water. A new forest. It is not that it was made just now. It is from beginningless time. Still the trees there, the creepers there, stay completely fresh. The water stays completely clean. The mountains are very beautiful. So he is using the word punya, sacred. Sacred mountains, sacred grass, sacred creepers. The mountains are supremely pure. The grass there is sacred. There is no useless thing like a foreign thorn-tree. No grass, creeper, or vine that does not serve. All of it is pure. Trees with beautiful fruit, trees with shade. Vrindavan is very good for the cowherds also. Good for the gopis to move about. And it is there for the cows. For everyone that place is very good. Upananda had already seen it all. Today Govardhana is its center. Such a mention comes later in another book. When those people went, they crossed the Yamuna. To the south of Kaliya's lake. Later the Yamuna, Kaliya's lake, was very wide. The whole community crossed. Not by a bridge. There were boats. They crossed by boat. And the cows crossed by swimming. That katha comes later. As we take it today, Vrindavan is not four kos. Vrindavan is the name of the whole circle of Braj. Especially leaving Barsana and Nandagaon to that side, and the boundary of Giriraj, and Khadiravan, Kumudavan, and the rest, all of these are inside Vrindavan. It is filled with a great expanse. There are very beautiful mountains. We will go there. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.28]

6
00:16:59,550 --> 00:20:34,990
Hearing this, all the elder cowherds said, excellent, very good, thank you. But when should we go. Then Upananda said, we shall go there this very day. Today. Now. Because so much fear was rising. When a tree is falling like this without a storm, any trouble can come. So as quickly as possible, we should flee. One acharya has written, however holy a place of pilgrimage in the world may be, the forest that protects Lord Krishna is Vrindavan. It is a forest that saves even the Lord. Such happiness had not come before, as came on seeing Vrindavan. The description will come ahead. There. Today itself we will go. Yoke the carts quickly. Put the nose-rope on the bulls of each cart, yoke the bulls. Let the loading of all the goods begin. Not, buy diesel and then we will go. There were bulls. A natural power. Now human society is becoming fully dependent on someone else. Then they were independent. The bullock cart is made from that same forest, and there is the bull. It only has to be yoked, and it will go. Do not delay. Go to your own houses and yoke them. And one more work. However many cows are spread in Mahavana, gather the cows in one place and send them ahead. Gathering the goods will take time. The going of the cows also takes time, because the cows will not go if you seize them in a rush. They will go eating and grazing on the way. This is the policy. They are our wealth. The wealth of cows. Cows are the greatest wealth. They are ours to protect. We have to protect them. They are our living. So drive the cows ahead. If this pleases you. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.29]

7
00:20:39,040 --> 00:24:18,150
Hearing this, the cowherds of one mind said, excellent, excellent. Very good, very good. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.30] They became ready to leave Mahavana. In truth, leaving Mahavana was not the main thing. The main thing was to come to Vrindavan. And this was the Lord's wish. Now they wanted to come to Vrindavan. Slowly they wanted to wander far, through the whole circle of Braj. In Mahavana there was a great goad, a control. They could not wander. There was not the age for wandering either. There was no strength in the body. So they also had the wish to come. Upananda spoke in this way, and all said it with one intelligence. Sanatana Goswami says, eka-dhi means the intelligence is in Krishna alone. They whose intelligence is fixed in one Krishna, they are of one intelligence. Everyone's intelligence was fixed in Krishna. So Krishna's protection alone was the aim before everyone. In leaving the place, in the trouble of the journey. No one said, Nanda Maharaj, what you say is right, but. No one said that. All accepted it in one voice. Come, come. All went to their own houses and began to yoke the carts. Nowadays it is very common, if, but. Srila Prabhupada made a war on this. Once someone was asking, and again and again he was putting if. Prabhupada says, why are you putting if. When you are giving knowledge, when you are saying a thing, why are you putting, if it is like this. When there is knowledge, speak it clear. This is the thing. Nowadays the custom of speaking is, yes, but something always comes. There it did not come. All said, all right. Not one person said, at least let some time be delayed, let us not go today, let me finish some work of mine. All right, we go today itself. They became ready. Because everyone's mind was fixed only on Krishna. One intelligence. And the welcome of his words, excellent, excellent.

8
00:24:51,920 --> 00:29:34,090
Whatever the people of Braj had, cloth, pots, ghee, milk, yogurt, in great jars, in many vessels, let them bring it all, let them keep it safe. Body, life, money, one's own people, all of it was for Krishna. Whatever the goods were, they were loading them on their own bullock carts. In truth all of it was for Krishna. Thinking this, they loaded it: Krishna takes happiness from this, this goods must be taken along. They yoked the carts and went. However many young cowherds there were, all were walking on foot, protecting. The elders, the children, and the women were seated on the bullock carts. They could not walk. This is a thing to learn. Elders, children, and women, and all the equipment, were loaded on the bullock carts. Well loaded. Loaded very well. So that nothing falls on the way. So that a soft thing is not crushed underneath. It was bound very well. The cows had already been sent ahead. Blowing the horn again and again, they called the cows. With a great sounding of horns they went, together with the priests. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.31-32] Blowing the horn, they were calling the cows. All were scattered. They stay busy grazing, so they stay scattered. The calves and the cows were brought together. That day this rule was kept. The cows were walking in joy. The children too were going, hopping. They went with the priests. A priest means one who does good from the front. One who stays in front and does the good. A priest is very necessary. The question will come, were the priests walking on foot. Some priests were such that they did not accept riding on a cart at all. They said, we will walk. And their need comes further. Those who were elders, they rode. The young priests walked along. Or a separate bullock cart will have been arranged for them also. However it was, they went.

9
00:29:36,240 --> 00:35:42,180
The gopis mounted the carts. He is describing the gopis separately. The gopis climbed onto the ratha. This ratha should not be taken as a chariot of horses. A chariot was brought there for the first time by Akrura. The people of the village saw for the first time what a chariot is. Before that they had not seen a chariot at all. This ratha is a bullock cart. The gopis climbed onto it. Shukadeva says, on their new cloth there was new kumkum. They were looking very beautiful. A necklace at the throat. A beautiful sari. And they sang Krishna's lila. They were singing in a sweet voice. The gopis mounted on the different bullock carts were singing Krishna's lila. The lilas that had happened in childhood, those they were singing. Without a drum, without a harmonium. The song is proving that they too are very happy. In joy they are going along singing Krishna's childhood lilas. With love. A great happiness was coming in the singing. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.33] Rohini and Yashoda were on one bullock cart. On it only a shade had been made. It had not been covered all around. They climbed taking Krishna and Balarama. They could not stay apart from Krishna and Balarama. The other small children were seated on different carts with the elders. Only the mothers of these two, on one cart, with these two. And it was placed in the middle of all the bullock carts. They were looking very beautiful. They were talking with each other. Sometimes a deer came out on the road, and Krishna would say, how many came out. Seeing some ordinary animal, he would say, it is a tiger. Balarama would say, no, it is not a tiger. Their speech was a lisp. The experience was still unfinished. But they kept saying something. How beautiful this tree is. This is, that is. Both mothers were eager to hear the talk of Krishna and Balarama. Those two were not speaking. When these two spoke with each other, or said something to Mother, especially seeing the forest on the road, seeing the animals. A new experience was coming. They are going to a new country. There is great happiness. They do not know where they are going. When the community goes somewhere, when there is a festival, children stay in happiness anyway. They do not have the grief that the village is being left. They were talking a great deal, looking and looking at the forest. Hearing that, the mothers were greatly delighted. Being with Krishna and Balarama, both mothers were looking very beautiful. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.34] Shukadeva Goswami also, by turns, is casting his eye on all the carts. He has seen the gopis. After that, Krishna and Balarama, Mother Rohini and Yashoda. Chiefly two carts. There is happiness that the people are coming to Vrindavan, so he is making this description.

10
00:35:44,320 --> 00:40:37,160
Having entered Vrindavan, which brings happiness in every season, they made a dwelling of Vraja there with the carts, like a half moon. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.35] All those cowherds, with the cows, entered Vrindavan properly. Before entering they had crossed the Yamuna. They could not come through Mathura. That is a city. How will cows walk in a city. There is trouble in walking through a city. Wherever they crossed. They came this side. Near Giriraj there is a bank, on the shore of the Yamuna. Shukadeva says, bringing happiness in every season. Giving happiness at all times. Which washes and brings it. Not every place gives happiness at all times. As in the days of heat people begin to flee to the Himalaya. Mussoorie, Dehradun. That gives happiness in the heat. In the winter it does not give that happiness. Going there, they arranged their own bullock carts. That itself was the house, in truth. Houses were not built yet. Coming into Vrindavan, they arranged the carts in the shape of a half moon. As the moon of the eighth day is, it was arranged like that. On all sides the bullock carts were gathered. The goods were on them. Sometimes they lived underneath them. In poverty a living had to be made, from the view of a dwelling. For the rest, for eating, drinking, and wearing, they were there. For putting on ornaments, those cowherds are full of ornaments. For eating and drinking there is nectar itself. Yogurt, ghee. For staying, there is the bullock cart. The real thing is food and drink. When food and drink are not good, what has happened by living in a house of gold. The splendor there. In itself the whole land is conscious, made of divine, non-material jewels. But even looking with an outer eye, Braj is very happy. A dwelling of Vraja. Its meaning is an enclosure. They began to make an enclosure. So that no one can enter from behind. Whoever comes, let him enter from the front. At first this was a forest. Later, when the six Goswamis and the acharyas came, the place became more. Now it has become a city, in the groves also, here also. At that time it was only a forest. With the carts alone they made the dwelling of Vraja. With the bullock carts, a half moon. They were doing this with great intelligence.

11
00:40:38,030 --> 00:47:29,000
They were walking with honor, protecting. A danger can be anywhere. Especially upon the cows, upon the children, and upon the women. They were walking while protecting them. A community should not become so weak that someone attacks and carries off a woman. Shukadeva says, weapons in hand. They were walking with bow and arrow. The people were walking, so they were doing their own protection themselves. Not that you hire someone, and that security will do it. They were able in protecting themselves. Able to face any enemy. This point is very necessary, whether the time is today or before. Especially upon wealth, upon a woman, and upon children, danger keeps coming. Many demons, or men of a wicked intention, who cast a bad eye. When the Lord went to the forest, neither of the two brothers was stolen. Sita was stolen away. Upon dear things the danger stays. Demons, people of wicked conduct, want to make such an attack. So the effort for protection. Then it becomes known what a husband is. When he cannot give his wife cloth, cannot build her a house, cannot give her an ornament, cannot protect her, then what is a husband. Sarbhanga says, he is a tea leaf. One should be able in protecting. In that, a man. The Lord went from Ayodhya, so what did he take. Only bow and arrow were with him. And with that he conquered Lanka, and he brought Sita back. What a husband's duty is, let the Lord teach by his own conduct. The wife who left the comfort of Ayodhya for her husband. Who did not keep her mind on the comfort of her father's house. Maharaj Dasharatha had said to Sumantra, bring the daughter Sita back. The father's house is there. If her wish is to stay in the father's house, let her stay there. A faithful wife, in this way. In a way she gave up the comfort of Janakpur, gave up the comfort of Ayodhya, and served Rama. Then the Lord in truth became so requested, so grateful, that for Sita he ground Lanka to dust. A husband should have one duty. And in truth a husband will do the protecting. Only courage is needed. The thought should be, we have to protect. He does it. So the cowherds walking here, protecting their children, their women, and their cows, are fully able. They are walking with weapons. Weapons are greatly needed. Peace does not come by saying peace, om shanti. Many times we have seen it in a sacrifice, om shanti and om shanti, and as the sacrifice is ending, the people doing the sacrifice end the sacrifice itself. A sacrifice is held for the peace of the world, and the pavilion of the sacrifice also becomes unpeaceful. In every age power is needed. Peace comes from power. Not from begging. Begging, the Lord was going to do. They had become ready for a breach. War is a necessary evil. It will happen. People who say war should not happen, peace, peace, are in truth cowards. They cannot establish peace. Peace was established by Guru Shivaji. These people had established peaces. But the people who join their hands only gave peace and went away. This has been seen. So walking here with bow and arrow was very necessary. Coming here, the mouth of the dwelling was on one side. All could know, if a danger is coming, it will come like this. If we answer, we will answer with the power of all together. So in that forest, in a new country, they made a dwelling in this way. Sri Krishna received a chance to wander, to see.

12
00:47:29,770 --> 00:51:14,000
Vrindavan, Govardhana, and the banks of the Yamuna. Seeing them, a supreme love arose in Rama and Madhava, O King. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.36] O Maharaj Parikshit, seeing Vrindavan, Govardhana, and the banks of the Yamuna, a supreme affection arose in the hearts of Rama and Madhava. Affection means happiness, joy. And supreme means the best of all. Until now Rama and Madhava had not received the happiness that came on seeing Vrindavan, Govardhana, and the sandy bank of the Yamuna. The Lord became much happier than before. For Krishna the word Madhava is given. Madhava also means the spring season. The double meaning is that spring also came and became happy in this forest. Or the husband of Lakshmi. Ma means Lakshmi, dhava means husband. The husband of Lakshmi. They experienced happiness in this forest. And the meaning is that even in Vaikuntha, staying with Lakshmi, the Lord had not received the happiness that came on seeing Vrindavan, Govardhana, and the Yamuna. The rasa raining in Braj, that happiness is not in Vaikuntha. The rasa raining in Barsana, that happiness is not in the three worlds. Even the Lord, condensed bliss, the husband of Lakshmi, began to be given happiness by the bank of the Yamuna. Such happiness he had not attained before. So they are saying supreme affection. The twelve forests, the bank of the Yamuna, Giriraj, and Vrindavan. All of these are inside Vrindavan. Still the sights are different. Seeing the different sights, a difference of affection is happening for the Lord. At Giriraj there is a happiness of one kind. On the bank of the Yamuna, another. In Vrindavan, another. In the Yamuna, the wish for water-play. Ahead they did the play of the water. They wandered on the bank, drank the water, played. In the caves of Giriraj they played hide and seek, they swung, they ate. There the happiness is of another kind. On the level land of Vrindavan, free wandering, a swing, the grazing of the cows, plays of many kinds. Blessed is this Vrindavan. This land is conscious. Everything is divine. For this reason, seeing all this, Krishna and Balarama have great happiness. By the word and, however many other forests there are, seeing the whole circle of Braj of eighty-four kos, Krishna has exceeding happiness, and Balarama also.

13
00:51:16,240 --> 00:59:27,440
In this way, giving happiness to the people of Braj, the Lord was doing the acts of a child. Giving affection to the people of Vraja by the acts of children and by sweet words, in their own time they became the keepers of the calves. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.37] Vrajaukas, those whose home is Braj, the cowherds, the cows, everyone, they were giving them affection by the acts of children and by sweet speech. The meaning of this affection and happiness should not be taken as the happiness we people find in the world. In the world there is no real happiness. Only when the mind is suited, we accept, this is happiness. But the happiness Krishna and Balarama were giving by their childhood lilas, by their sweet words, was in truth a conscious happiness. The bliss of their own form. The happiness of the soul. The bliss that comes face to face from the Lord, that bliss was coming to the soul. And that supreme affection was happening to Krishna and Balarama also. In this happiness the two of them, by various childhood lilas, were giving happiness to Braj. Kala means sweet. Like a sweet cry. Sweet speech. And the acts of a child. According to their own time, they now became keepers of the calves. They had not yet become keepers of the cows. They became keepers of the calves of the cows. They were small children. So they received permission to take the small calves of the cows a little way. Calves will not stay tied to the post. They also have to be grazed. The cows go far. The calves wander a little way. They do not need a great deal of grazing, because by evening half the stomach is filled with milk. They drink the cow's milk. Some need of grass still remains. It is better than milk alone that they also eat grass and also drink milk. The body stays well. So Krishna and Balarama received permission to keep the calves. They became keepers of the calves. What they spoke in a lisp, that itself gave the joy. Ahead the lisp ended, but the throat was such that even talk sounded like singing. Any raga. He would speak a bird's call, and even a parrot would be shy.

14
00:59:28,880 --> 01:04:07,780
On this Srila Prabhupada writes that Krishna and Balarama had taken birth in so great a family, almost in a king's house. But still they were not sent to school. Brahmanas were taught according to the Veda and scripture. The settling of various truths, the truth of the Lord, ayurveda, astrology. Kshatriyas, the science of the bow. If the dear boy is good, he was taught the science of war. Vaishyas, trade, farming, and the protection of the cow. Shudras, service, driving a cart, building a house, and however many works. This training is given from the beginning. Prabhupada says, Sri Krishna is a vaishya, therefore the protection of the cow. Jiva Goswami writes that at that time they were two and a half years. Somewhere it is said two years and four months. In a commentary two years is written. Whatever the print. Even three and a half years is not so much. When they became three and a half, they became keepers of the calves. A degree was received. The meaning is that the strength of their body, the intelligence, developed somewhat. They could keep calves. Where did they keep them. Not far from the land of Vraja. Right near the land of Braj. This Braj, on the bank, where the village had been settled by the bullock carts. They called the village Vraja, and the forest the forest. Only a little way from their own village. With the boys of the cowherds. With the children of the other cowherds, very near the village, grazing the calves. They grazed the calves, they took them about. With many playthings. Both had articles of play of many kinds. They would sound the ankle bells. Mostly they kept playing. In truth, playing itself was the main thing for Krishna and Balarama. And keeping the calves was only an instrument. In it a chance to play was found. They would go along grazing. Many playthings. Paricchada means the articles. As they would take a flute, they would take a horn, and a ball. Grazing the calves did not stay very difficult. They kept articles of play of many kinds. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.38]

15
01:04:11,780 --> 01:12:01,310
How they played, Shukadeva is describing that. Sometimes they would begin to play the flute. The description is of both brothers playing the flute together. Not by turns. But it should be understood by itself, it was not necessary that both play at the same time. Sometimes several cowherds played together, and there was a contest, who can play well. A special description of Sri Krishna's flute-playing is not being made yet. They have not yet learned it well. He is the holder of the flute, but he will learn the playing ahead. He does not yet know that much art. If he played in a special way, Shukadeva would describe him separately. Now he will be learning to blow. He has not yet learned to move the fingers. In the beginning Sri Krishna could not play a flute uncommon among the other children. So there is no special description. While one remains a child, the intelligence does not develop. Whatever there is, it should be put in the mouth. Slowly they will have learned. Sometimes playing the flute. Striking with pebbles. Sometimes a throw with a pebble. And a clod. They would take a contest across the river. They would throw a wide tile or a stone over the water. It would go over the water. Our tile went this far. The children become very happy. This is called ksepana. Or they throw a clod to pick fruit from some tree. Sometimes with the feet, with the ankle bells. There was a contest in sounding the anklets bound on the feet. Both brothers, Krishna and Balarama, would sound the anklets, dance, and say, I am playing better. With the small children, the little bells. The ones that stay at the waist. And sometimes, artificial bulls. They would fight, becoming make-believe bulls. Becoming cattle and fighting. Someone is a black bull, so putting on a black blanket they would roar, bellow, speak like a bull. Or some wood, like a horn. Acting as bulls, roaring, they would fight each other. Becoming bulls, bellowing like a bull, hissing, and fighting each other. The two brothers would fight in this way, would fill themselves, would learn. These same children will ahead become skilled fighters. All this is being learned. One day the Lord, the seven bulls, for Nagnajit's daughter Satya. That person will take her hand who subdues the seven bulls. Those bulls were such that each bull had broken the hands of thousands of heroes, had broken their legs. Even one was hard to catch. Maharaj Nagnajit said, you are the Lord of all, the husband of Lakshmi, in every way a suitable husband for my daughter. Still I have kept this condition. You can fulfill it. The description for the Lord is that he bound his waist, the yellow cloth at the hip, and he put the rope in their noses. Then the Lord's opulence happened. So the Lord had learned this art from the beginning. In himself he is eternally full of every art. But from a worldly view, this training of Sri Krishna became very good. Birds, insects, he learned their calls. He would speak a call such that sometimes the confusion came, a bird is speaking. Imitating the cuckoo's kuhu-kuhu, even the cuckoo would sometimes be shy. Like two material children, two children of the ordinary human community, going along doing lila here and there, going along imitating. The two of them went along doing so. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.39-40]

16
01:12:03,230 --> 01:17:42,760
Now hear, a special event is about to happen. Once, on the bank of the Yamuna, as the two were grazing the calves with their own friends of the same age, a demon came, desiring to kill Krishna and Balarama. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.41] At some time, on the bank of the Yamuna, the two brothers, with their friends, of the same age, their friends dearer than life, were grazing the calves. A demon came to kill those two. From Kamsa the order always stayed, go, kill Krishna and Balarama. Many demons would come. Whoever came into Braj, they came changing disguise. In a hidden form. They were dearer to Krishna than life, so the word svaka is used. His own friends. How fortunate those children will be, who received the Lord in the form of a friend. All human beings receive companions in childhood. At that time they were not playing. They were grazing the calves. Sometimes, stopping the play, they would graze with special care. That time has come. Now the milking, they have to be taken along. Into the herd of calves a demon sent by Kamsa had mixed. Taking the form of a calf, he had entered it. Hari, seeing that demon in the form of a calf, gone into the herd of calves, showing him to Baladeva, slowly approached as if bewildered. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.42] Sri Krishna, who takes the life of demons. Seeing that demon in the form of a calf, gone into the herd, he tells Sri Balarama quietly. This calf. They recognize who it is. The Lord is a small child, and he points, this is a new calf. Everyone knows. But he is asking like one bewildered. He shows. Whom is he showing. Baladeva. There the need of Rama did not come. The need of strength came, in order to kill. Like one bewildered, like one who does not know, I know this is only a calf. And he went up to him. Without fear. The calf was understanding, an ignorant child has come to me. He was in the turn of killing him. Taking the two hind legs, together with the tail. The Lord thought, if I catch only the two legs, he will strike my face with the tail. So he caught both the tail and the legs. With both hands. Having caught, he began to whirl him. A small child began to whirl him. And just as he came to the condition of dying, at that same time he became heavy as a mountain. Still the Lord is whirling him. He came into his own form. And the Lord is infallible. While he was whirling that demon, still in the form of a calf, the Lord did not move one step. He was not shaken this way and that. So the word achyuta. As he was, so he began to whirl. All the children began to watch the show. A child of three and a half years is doing a wonder like this. Having whirled him, he threw the dead body onto the top of a kapittha tree. He died from the whirling itself. After that he threw him with great force onto the kapittha tree. His body had become vast, so the kapittha fell from his blow, and from the blow of that kapittha several more kapittha trees fell. That great-bodied one, being thrown, fell. He had become great-bodied. He could not hide his disguise. When the Lord struck, he came into his own form. The body was very large. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.43]

17
01:17:44,740 --> 01:22:17,510
Seeing him, the boys were astonished, excellent, excellent. Seeing that he was a demon, and that Krishna had killed him, all the children were greatly pleased. Here the children were praising him. And from above the gods began to rain flowers. The gods, fully satisfied, became showers of flowers. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.44] The gods began to rain flowers, began to offer praise. Here all the children are greatly pleased. In this way, this is the first lila in Vrindavan of killing a demon. So in Braj the Lord has not killed any demon with a weapon. Someone with a kick, someone with the hand, someone with the lip, into someone's mouth he put his hand. He kills in this way and that. Mostly with the hand, with the foot. He had taken nothing in the hand. A child of three and a half years, and he kills a demon this large by whirling him overhead. Still foolish people do not accept Sri Krishna as the Lord. Now what intelligence should be fitted to them. What thing should be told to the wicked. The evildoers, the bewildered, the lowest of men, do not surrender to Me. [Bhagavad-gita 7.15] The Lord says in the Gita, people who are very low, who are wicked, whose intelligence is spoiled, they do not take shelter of Me. Shelter they do take, but of others, of one whose shelter should not be taken. Becoming a man's spoon, they flatter him. But they do not worship the Lord. There are many people of this kind in the world, who do not accept the Lord. The two of them, having become keepers of calves, the only maintainers of all the worlds. Having taken the morning meal, grazing the calves. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.45] Those two, Krishna and Balarama, what are they. They are the only maintainers of all the worlds. But today, under the love of the devotees, they have become keepers of calves. Or, the two brothers, having become keepers of calves, became the maintainers of all the worlds. They are killing demons, so they will maintain the gods. The gods maintain the world. Even while being keepers of calves, they are maintaining all the worlds. This is the meaning. One meaning: they are keeping calves, but in this itself they killed the demon, so they did the work of the gods. And the gods maintain the world. In this way the Lord is maintaining all the worlds.

18
01:22:22,630 --> 01:28:08,850
Eating at six or seven, driving them at half past seven or eight, this is not the intention of the saying. They would wake even at six. Those children did not wake in the very early morning. Only one day they went out completely early, and they had not eaten. The day of Aghasura. Blowing the horn, he woke all the children, and completely early he went taking the children. That day Brahma was to be put into bewilderment. But on the other days, the morning meal. They would go after eating in the morning. They would keep grazing the calves. They would wander in the morning. Now another lila. Each his own herd of calves. Once, going near a lake. Having let them drink, they drank the water. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.46] One day all the cowherd children went, each taking his own herd of calves. Not to the bank of the Yamuna. To a lake. Here the Yamuna is not written. They went to some lake, to give water. This is east of Nandagaon, some place, where the Lord killed Bakasura. Giving them drink. The calves had become thirsty. Having given them drink. He had given the calves water. Then they drank the water. Then they themselves drank. How well mannered, and how well taught, the children are. They know that we are the servants of the calves. So first they gave the calves water, then they drank water. Animals also should be given water on time. People of now have no such training. They also should be given water on time, should be fed on time. What an ideal this is. Let this be called a keeper of calves. On waking in the morning, the tea starts at once. Just drink, eat. One's own stove goes up. And with great difficulty it is sometimes remembered that there is also a calf in the house, and a cow, and it also needs food. Look, they are the children of cowherds. They are children of three and three and a half years. But they first gave the calves water, then they drank. This is dharma. This art of keeping cows, this rule, should be learned. People of now eat themselves, and after eating they throw the leftover to the cow. Then they consider that very much, we have done a great piety. They gave it their leftover water and their leftover. That alone became enough. Still, those people also are pious. At least they keep a cow. Then some people are such fools that they keep a dog, and they do not keep a cow at all. Their life is going in the raising of the dog alone. Early in the morning they go out taking it for a walk. They stay busy in the service of the dog. The dog passes stool, so they look there, they wait, they are having the master pass stool. They wander, taking it. They cannot keep a cow. They keep a dog, without a purpose. All these are marks of a low consciousness. So it should be learned, to be a keeper of calves, to be a keeper of cows. Keeping cows should be the dharma of every person. So the Lord, having given the calves water, then drank water. From all these points we should learn.

19
01:28:08,850 --> 01:31:22,950
When the children had drunk the water, and after that the children had drunk the water, near that lake, where they were drinking water, right near it an astonishing creature was seen. It was an astonishing creature. It was in the shape of a baka. In the shape of a crane. But a crane this large had not been seen. It was as high as some mountain peak thrown down by a thunderbolt, and heavy. And completely white. White feathers. Sitting in meditation, white feathers. And it was showing, I am completely in the mode of goodness. One acharya says, those who look completely bright from the outside, and inside there is no worship of the Lord. What, they want to eat the Lord. The Lord tears them, rips them, into pieces. So what was the crane doing. It keeps saying bak, bak. The crane keeps chattering. So it was called baka. You have heard, baka. According to the pronunciation, according to the call, it was given the name. And to other birds also. A crow is called kaka. It keeps saying caw, caw. The crane keeps speaking a very ugly, thick word. But the crane's meditation is very famous. It stands on one leg in a lake, or on the bank of a river, and it practices meditation. Meditation on what. On the fish. That it come near. And that is all. It has a great beak. It strikes with a gulp, and it eats. Then the meditation continues. And the fish do not come to know that a babaji is meditating here. They keep coming. They understand, some wood is standing, or some piece of stone. The meditation is that still. It meditates, taking the silence of wood. Even now anyone can see it. In truth its standing is motionless. So the Lord has given this intelligence to all living beings, for obtaining their own food. Then that demon had come in the form of a crane. He had heard, he was Putana's brother. A full brother of the same womb. Aghasura also was Putana's brother. Brothers and sisters of this kind were in that family, who are going to kill the Lord. What can be done. All had fallen into bad company. They had fallen into the company of Kamsa. This sitting stops here.
